# krume.identity
# Krume - neighborhood sourdough bakery, Berlin-Neukölln.
# This file is the reference implementation for Brand Intent.
# Every decision is commented to explain its derivation.

# ── Positioning ──
# Krume exists because its founder believes bread is craft,
# not lifestyle. The bakery is small, the hours are early,
# the sourdough is slow. Nothing about this is "curated."

essence:  Bread is craft, not lifestyle. Flour, water, time.

promise:  Walk in and you smell at once that someone has been
          standing here since four in the morning.

tagline:  Flour, water, time.  # also known as "claim" (DACH)

positioning:  Krume is the only bakery in the Kiez that names every
              bread by its flour, not by its marketing.

mission:  Honest bread for the Kiez: every day, from the same oven,
          with the same hands.

vision:  A neighbourhood where nobody has to explain any more
         why good bread costs six euros.

archetype: craftsman

# ── Narratives ──
# Named stories. Exactly one carries `primary`: the default story
# for about pages, editorial, press. The others serve specific goals.

narrative Flour Water Time
  primary
  Founded in 2019, when the baker quit her agency job
  to do what she had done every morning since she was
  sixteen: bake bread. The shop on Weserstraße used to
  be a copy shop. The first week, the oven was too small.
  The second week, the bread sold out too fast.
  Neither has changed.

narrative Eighteen Hours
  The bread takes eighteen hours. No step is cut short,
  no flour is swapped. Three kinds a day, not thirty.
  What's gone is gone; tomorrow there's more.

narrative Weserstraße
  Weserstraße has changed. Rents go up, the Spätis
  disappear. Krume stays. Not because we are pushing
  against anything, but because the oven is on at four
  and the regulars are at the door at seven.
  That is the whole resistance.

# ── Voice ──
# The voice is the baker herself: direct, dry, proud of the work.
# Not warm-and-fuzzy. Not educational. Not performative.
# She talks the way she'd talk to someone across the counter.

voice
  register:  informal, first names
  persona:   The baker behind the counter: direct, dry, proud of the craft
  rhythm:    short sentences, concrete facts, no explaining

  # always/never: concrete, testable rules.
  # An AI agent can evaluate any text against these with a yes/no answer.

  always:
    - sourdough, not "artisan bread"
    - named flours (Tipo 1, rye 1150) over "the finest ingredients"
    - fermentation in hours, not "slow-fermented"
    - the Kiez by name (Neukölln, Weserstraße) over "our location"
    - the weight in grams on every bread

  never:
    - lifestyle language (curated, hand-picked, moment of indulgence)
    - "artisan" or "artisanal": we say craft
    - food-blog words where a baker's word exists (crumb structure, flavour profile)
    - superlatives without substance (best bread, unique taste)
    - wellness or health claims (good for you, clean eating)

  # instead: the phrase to avoid and what Krume writes instead.
  # A pair teaches the rewrite, not just the ban.

  instead:
    - "the finest ingredients" -> Tipo 1 and rye 1150 from the mill in Dachau
    - "baked with love" -> eighteen hours of fermentation
    - "fresh from the oven" -> on the shelf since six

# ── Values ──
# Not keywords - behavior statements. Each value describes
# how it manifests in what we say and do.

values
  Craft
    We name the process, not the result.
    Fermentation in hours, the flour by name.
    No bread without a traceable way from grain to loaf.
    say: The rye has been proofing since last night.
    not: Handmade with love.

  Honesty
    No bread without an ingredient list. No promises
    the dough can't keep. When something didn't work,
    we say so.
    say: No spelt today. The dough wouldn't.
    not: Sold out, sorry! Come back tomorrow!

  Kiez
    The shop belongs to Weserstraße like the Späti
    next door. We know the regulars by name and
    the neighbours by their bread.

# ── Content Pillars ──
# What Krume talks about, and what it explicitly avoids.

pillars
  primary:   sourdough, craft, ingredients, Kiez
  secondary: seasonal, sustainability, baking know-how
  avoid:     lifestyle gastronomy, wellness eating, influencer culture

# ── Audiences ──
# Two audiences with genuinely different language needs.
# The regulars don't need explanations. The newcomers do -
# but never in a condescending way.

audience regulars
  label:       Regulars from the Kiez
  profile:     25-65, daily or weekly, know the breads
  motivation:  their bread, their shop, a reliable rhythm
  language:    brief, direct, no explaining: they know

audience newcomers
  label:       The curious and passers-by
  profile:     tourists, new neighbours, market visitors
  motivation:  find good bread, understand what is different
  language:    inviting, concrete, explains without lecturing

# ── Anti-Audiences ──
# Explicitly not-our-people. Gives the AI a concrete
# "never sound like you're talking to this person" signal.

anti-audience influencers
  label:       Lifestyle influencers
  description: People who post bread as an aesthetic instead of
               eating it as food. We are not their content.

anti-audience discount-hunters
  label:       Bargain hunters
  description: Anyone comparing the price of a sourdough loaf with
               supermarket toast is in the wrong place. We are happy
               to explain why it costs what it costs,
               but we don't justify it.

# ── Imagery ──
# The image counterpart of voice: yes/no rules for photos and film.
# Framing and colour treatment live in krume.brand.

imagery
  always:
    - real steps of the work in the bakehouse, nothing staged
    - flour, dough and tools with signs of use
    - daylight or the light of the bakehouse
  never:
    - posed smiles into the camera
    - bread as decoration on linen and wooden boards
    - AI-generated or AI-altered images

image-theme Four in the Morning
  The bakehouse before the shop opens: hands in dough,
  flour on the bench, the oven open.

image-theme Across the Counter
  Regulars and passers-by at the counter,
  bread changing hands, Weserstraße in the background.

# ── Goals ──
# What a post is for, independent of its format.
# Shares: monthly target for Instagram.

goal craft
  label:    Show the craft
  share:    60%
  question: How is the bread made?
  narratives: Eighteen Hours
  always:
    - name one concrete step, one flour or one time

goal kiez
  label:    Kiez
  share:    30%
  question: Who comes here, and why?
  narratives: Weserstraße
  always:
    - a person with their first name and their reason to come

goal service
  label:    Service
  share:    10%
  question: What is there today, when is it open?
  always:
    - name the day, the time and the bread

# ── Review ──
# Asked before anything goes out. Each one is a yes/no question.

review
  copy:
    - Does the text name the bread, the flour, the weight or the fermentation time?
    - Does it sound like the baker behind the counter?
  image:
    - Does the picture show a real step of the work or real customers?
    - Is the bread food, not decoration?
